  "description": "E01 with freshness checking left at its default, against entries that are past their expiry.",
  "note": "The same envelope as E01, differing only in that expiry is checked. The reference verifier short-circuits on freshness: it marks the entry expired and returns before the signature is examined at all (multi-attest-verify.js, the expiry branch that precedes verifySignature). So signatureValid reads false on both slots here, not because either signature is bad, but because neither was checked. E01 is the same two entries with freshness off and both verify, which is how you can tell. The consequence worth knowing: in this envelope format an expired entry and a forged one are indistinguishable in signatureValid, and error is the only field that separates them, carrying \"Attestation expired\" rather than a signature failure. A relying party that needs to tell a stale attestation from a fabricated one must read error rather than the boolean. Any published fixture is permanently past its window, which is why E01 to E05 and E07 pin checkExpiry off and this one does not.",
